use std
into ole_test.amni

```amni
import os;
import dm;
import ole;
import cfbmk;
```

`emit(text const byte[:])` prints text one line at a time with `os::write_stdout`: for each line (split at the newline byte 10, ignoring the empty piece after a final newline) it prints the line itself followed by a newline, except that an empty line is printed as a single dot "." followed by a newline.

`dump(name const byte[:], ok bool)` prints a line "== " and the name, then the Markdown from `dm::dm_markdown` (written into a scratch list and printed with `emit`), then one line "meta " plus key, an equals sign and value for each line of `dm::dm_meta` (the key and value are separated by the tab byte 9 there), then one line "warn " plus the line for each line of `dm::dm_warn`, and when ok is false one line "reader returned false".

`show(name const byte[:], kind const byte[:], unit const byte[:])` reads the file "tests/fixtures/" followed by name with `os::read_file` (printing "missing" and returning when it cannot be read), calls `dm::dm_reset(kind, unit)`, then `dump(name, ole::ol_read(data))`.

`le(out byte[>..]&, v int, n int)` appends v to out as n little-endian bytes.

`txt(out byte[>..]&, s const byte[:], wide bool)` appends s to out: each byte of s as one byte when wide is false, and as a 2-byte little-endian unit when wide is true.

`rec(out byte[>..]&, id int, data const byte[:])` appends a BIFF record to out: `le` of id (2 bytes), `le` of the length of data (2 bytes), then data.

`bof(out byte[>..]&, ver int, ty int)` appends the record 0x0809 whose 16 data bytes are `le` of ver (2), of ty (2) and 12 zero bytes.

`cell(out byte[>..]&, id int, row int, col int, ixfe int, tail const byte[:])` appends the record id whose data is `le` of row, col and ixfe (2 bytes each) followed by tail.

`numc(out byte[>..]&, row int, col int, ixfe int, hi int, lo int)` appends a NUMBER cell (id 0x0203) whose 8 value bytes are `le` of lo (4 bytes) then of hi (4 bytes).

`rkc(out byte[>..]&, row int, col int, ixfe int, rk int)` appends an RK cell (id 0x027E) whose value is `le` of rk (4 bytes).

`labc(out byte[>..]&, row int, col int, s const byte[:])` appends a LABEL cell (id 0x0204) with ixfe 0 whose tail is `le` of the length of s (2 bytes), a flags byte 0 and s as 8-bit characters.

`sstc(out byte[>..]&, row int, col int, n int)` appends a LABELSST cell (id 0x00FD) with ixfe 0 whose tail is `le` of n (4 bytes).

`xf(out byte[>..]&, ifmt int)` appends an XF record (id 0x00E0) of 20 bytes: 2 zero bytes, `le` of ifmt (2 bytes), then 16 zero bytes.

`fmt(out byte[>..]&, id int, code const byte[:])` appends a FORMAT record (id 0x041E): `le` of id (2), `le` of the length of code (2), a flags byte 0 and code as 8-bit characters.

`bsheet(out byte[>..]&, off int, state int, ty int, name const byte[:])` appends a BOUNDSHEET record (id 0x0085): `le` of off (4), the bytes state and ty, one byte holding the length of name, a flags byte 0 and the name as 8-bit characters.

`sstblock(out byte[>..]&)` appends the shared string table of the workbook tests as four records. The first, id 0x00FC, has data: `le` of 7 (4 bytes, total count), `le` of 7 (4 bytes, unique count), then strings: "plain" (`le` of 5 in 2 bytes, flags 0, the 5 characters); then "alpha beta gamma" cut inside its characters: `le` of 16 (2), flags 0, and just the first 6 characters "alpha ". The second record, id 0x003C, has data: a flags byte 0, the remaining 10 characters "beta gamma", then the string "caf" cut after 3 characters: `le` of 11 (2), flags 0 and "caf". The third, id 0x003C, has data: a flags byte 1, the 8 UTF-16 code units of "é Привет" as `le` values of 0xE9, 0x20, 0x41F, 0x440, 0x438, 0x432, 0x435, 0x442 (2 bytes each), then a rich string: `le` of 4 (2), flags 8, `le` of 1 (2, run count), the 4 characters "rich" and 4 bytes of run data (all zero); then an extended string: `le` of 3 (2), flags 4, `le` of 3 (4, phonetic length), the characters "ext" and 3 bytes of phonetic data (all zero). The fourth, id 0x003C, has data that starts directly with the string "after" (`le` of 5 in 2 bytes, flags 0, the 5 characters) with no flags byte.

`cellsa(out byte[>..]&)` appends these cells of the first sheet: row 0, columns 0 to 5: `sstc` of strings 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 (a label cell is not used there). Row 1: `rkc` in column 0 with ixfe 1 and rk 10 (the date 2 in a date format), `rkc` in column 1 with ixfe 2 and rk 16400303 (41000.75 in a custom date format), `rkc` in column 2 with ixfe 3 and rk 10 (2 in a plain format), `numc` in column 3 with ixfe 4 and hi 0x3FF00000, lo 0 (1.0 in a boolean format), `numc` in column 4 with ixfe 4 and hi 0, lo 0 (0.0 in a boolean format), `numc` in column 5 with ixfe 0 and hi 0x401C0000, lo 0 (7.0). Row 2: a MULRK record (id 0x00BD) with data `le` of 2 (row, 2 bytes), `le` of 0 (first column, 2 bytes), then three entries of ixfe 0 (2 bytes) and an RK (4 bytes): 22, 5003 and 0x3FE00000, then `le` of 2 (last column, 2 bytes); and an RK cell in column 3 with the negative integer minus 3 as the RK 0xFFFFFFF6. Row 3: BOOLERR cells (id 0x0205, tail the value byte and then the error byte): column 0 value 1 and 0, column 1 value 0 and 0, column 2 value 0x2A and 1, column 3 value 0x24 and 1.

`cellsb(out byte[>..]&)` appends these cells of the first sheet: row 4: FORMULA cells (id 0x0006, tail: 8 result bytes, then 2 bytes of flags, 4 bytes of chain and `le` of 0 (2 bytes, no tokens)): column 0 with result 7.0 (`le` of 0 in 4 bytes then `le` of 0x401C0000 in 4 bytes); column 1 with the special result bytes 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 255, 255 (a string), immediately followed by a STRING record (id 0x0207) whose data is `le` of 3 (2), flags 0 and "str"; column 2 with the special result 1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 255, 255 (a boolean true); column 3 with 2, 0, 0x17, 0, 0, 0, 255, 255 (an error); column 4 with 3, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 255, 255 (an empty string); then `labc` in column 5 with the text "t" followed by a tab byte and "x". Then a nested chart: `bof(out, 0x600, 0x20)`, `sstc` of row 4 column 6 and string 0, and the end record (id 0x000A, no data); the nested cell must not show up. Row 5 is left out. Row 6 as the file holds it: `labc` of row 6 column 2 "c", then `labc` of row 6 column 0 "a", then `labc` of row 6 column 0 "dup". Row 8 before row 7: `labc` of row 8 column 0 "eight", then `labc` of row 7 column 0 "seven". Row 9: a BLANK record (id 0x0201, tail empty, ixfe 0, column 0) and `labc` of row 9 column 1 "z".

`wbglobals(out byte[>..]&, d1904 bool, o1 int, o2 int)` appends the workbook globals: `bof(out, 0x600, 5)`, a DATEMODE record (id 0x0022) with `le` of 1 or 0 (2 bytes) by d1904, the `fmt` records 164 "yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm", 165 "0.00" and 166 holding the text of the three words TRUE, TRUE and FALSE each in double quotes and joined by semicolons, five `xf` records with ifmt 0, 14, 164, 165 and 166, `bsheet` records: offset o1 state 0 type 0 "Data"; offset o2 state 1 type 0 "Hid & Den"; offset 0 state 0 type 2 "Chart", then `sstblock`, and the end record (id 0x000A, no data).

`wbbuild(d1904 bool, out byte[>..]&)` clears out and builds a BIFF8 workbook: first the first sheet in a scratch list a (`bof(a, 0x600, 0x10)`, `cellsa`, `cellsb`, the end record) and the second in b (`bof(b, 0x600, 0x10)`, `sstc` of row 0 column 0 and string 0, the end record); then the globals in a scratch list g with `wbglobals(g, d1904, 0, 0)` to learn its length; then clear g and `wbglobals(g, d1904, g's old length, g's old length plus the length of a)`; out is g followed by a and b.

`cfile(name const byte[:], stream const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` clears out and builds a compound file holding one stream, called name, with `cfbmk::cm_reset`, `cfbmk::cm_add(name, stream)` and `cfbmk::cm_build(false, 109, out)`.

`runx(label const byte[:], name const byte[:], stream const byte[:])` builds `cfile` of name and stream, calls `dm::dm_reset("xls", "sheet")` and `dump(label, ole::ol_read(file))`.

`bigbook(out byte[>..]&)` clears out and builds a BIFF8 workbook with one sheet "Big" of 50001 rows: a scratch list a holds `bof(a, 0x600, 0x10)`, then for rows 0 to 50000 a `numc` in column 0 with ixfe 0 and hi 0x401C0000, lo 0, then the end record (id 0x000A, no data); the globals g are `bof(g, 0x600, 5)`, a `bsheet` at offset 0 first to learn the length, then the real one: `bsheet(g, length of the globals, 0, 0, "Big")` and the end record; out is g followed by a. The globals are built twice with a fresh g, the first time with offset 0, to know their length.

`lockedbook(out byte[>..]&)` clears out and builds a workbook whose globals are `bof(out, 0x600, 5)`, a FILEPASS record (id 0x002F, data `le` of 1 (2 bytes) and 4 zero bytes), a `bsheet` with offset 0, state 0, type 0 and name "Secret", and the end record (id 0x000A, no data).

`oldbook(out byte[>..]&)` clears out and builds a BIFF5 workbook (8-bit strings): the globals g are `bof(g, 0x500, 5)`, a FORMAT record (id 0x041E) with `le` of 164 (2), a 1-byte length and the text "yyyy-mm-dd", two XF records (id 0x00E0, 16 bytes each: 2 zero bytes, `le` of ifmt (2), 12 zero bytes) with ifmt 0 and 164, a BOUNDSHEET (id 0x0085) with `le` of the offset (4), the bytes 0 and 0, a 1-byte length and the name "Old" (8-bit, no flags byte), and the end record; the sheet a holds `bof(a, 0x500, 0x10)`, a LABEL cell (id 0x0204, row 0, column 0, ixfe 0, tail `le` of 4 (2) then "caf" and the byte 0xE9, no flags byte), an RK cell in row 0, column 1 with ixfe 1 and rk 10 (the date 2), a NUMBER cell in row 1, column 0 with ixfe 0 and hi 0x40040000, lo 0, and the end record. As with the other workbook builders, build g twice to learn its length for the offset; out is g followed by a.

`wdbuild(nfib int, flags int, tabname const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` clears out and builds a compound file holding a Word document. The table stream t is the byte 2, `le` of the length of the piece table (4 bytes) and the piece table: `le` of 0, 19 and 70 (4 bytes each, the character positions), then two 8-byte descriptors: `le` of 0 (2), `le` of 0x40001000 (4), `le` of 0 (2) and `le` of 0 (2), `le` of 0x900 (4), `le` of 0 (2). The main stream w holds, in this order: `le` of 0xA5EC (2), of nfib (2), 6 zero bytes, `le` of flags (2), zero bytes until w is 0x4C long, `le` of 70 (4), zero bytes until w is 0x98 long, `le` of 80 (2), zero bytes until w is 0x1A2 long, `le` of 0 (4) and of the length of t (4), zero bytes until w is 0x800 long, the 19 bytes of the first piece: "Caf", the byte 0xE9, a space, the byte 0x93, "q", the byte 0x94, a space, the byte 0x80, " 5", the byte 13 and "Hello ", zero bytes until w is 0x900 long, then the second piece as 51 UTF-16 units (`le` of each, 2 bytes): the text "world" (use `txt` with wide true) then 13, then the 6 units 0x41F, 0x440, 0x438, 0x432, 0x435, 0x442 then 13, then 0x13, the text " HYPERLINK " (wide), 0x14, "link" (wide), 0x15, " end" (wide), 13, then the cells 65 ("A"), 7, 66 ("B"), 7, 7, 67 ("C"), 7, 68 ("D"), 7, 7, then "Last" (wide) and 13. Build the compound file with `cfbmk::cm_reset`, `cfbmk::cm_add("WordDocument", w)`, `cfbmk::cm_add(tabname, t)` and `cfbmk::cm_build(false, 109, out)`.

`prc(out byte[>..]&, ver int, inst int, ty int, data const byte[:])` appends a PowerPoint record: `le` of ver plus 16 times inst (2 bytes), `le` of ty (2), `le` of the length of data (4), then data.

`ptext(out byte[>..]&, kind int, wide bool, s const byte[:])` appends a text header record (type 0x0F9F, version 0, instance 0, data `le` of kind (4 bytes)) and then a text record: type 0x0FA0 when wide (s as UTF-16, by `txt` with wide true) or type 0x0FA8 (s as bytes) when not.

`pbox(out byte[>..]&, ph int, body const byte[:])` appends a shape: a record of type 0xF004 and version 15 whose data is, when ph is above 0, a record of type 0xF011 (version 15) holding a record of type 0x0BC3 whose 8 data bytes are `le` of 0 (4), the byte ph and 3 zero bytes; then a record of type 0xF00D (version 15) holding body.

`pdraw(out byte[>..]&, shapes const byte[:])` appends a record of type 0x040C (version 15) holding a record of type 0xF002 (version 15) holding a record of type 0xF003 (version 15) holding shapes.

`ppbuild(token int, out byte[>..]&)` clears out and builds the compound file of a presentation with two streams. In the stream d ("PowerPoint Document"), written in this order, with offsets taken from the length of d when each part starts: (1) the document: a record of type 0x03E8 (version 15) holding two list records of type 0x0FF0 (version 15): the first with instance 0 holding a persist atom (type 0x03F3, 20 bytes: `le` of 2, 0, 0 and 256, then 4 zero bytes, each 4 bytes), `ptext(0, true, "Title one")`, `ptext(1, false, ...)` of the bytes "First point", 13, "Second", the byte 0xE9 and " point", a style record (type 0x0FA1, data: a paragraph run of `le` of 12 (4), `le` of 0 (2), `le` of 0 (4); another run of `le` of 13 (4), `le` of 1 (2), `le` of 0 (4); then a character run of `le` of 25 (4) and `le` of 0 (4)), a persist atom with `le` of 3, 0, 0 and 257 and 4 zero bytes (each 4 bytes), and `ptext(0, true, "Second slide")`; the second list with instance 2 holding a persist atom with `le` of 4, 0, 0 and 257 and 4 zero bytes, a persist atom with `le` of 5, 0, 0 and 256 and 4 zero bytes, and `ptext(2, true, "Remember the demo")`. (2) The first slide at offset s1: a record of type 0x03EE (version 15) holding a record of type 0x03EF with 24 zero bytes and `pdraw` of the shapes `pbox(0, ...)` with body a record 0x0F9E of 4 bytes (`le` of 0), `pbox(0, ...)` with `ptext(4, true, "Free box")`, `pbox(9, ...)` with `ptext(4, true, "Footer text")` and `pbox(7, ...)` with `ptext(4, true, "Date text")`. (3) The second slide at offset s2: the same record layout with one shape `pbox(0, ...)` with `ptext(4, true, "Old text")`. (4) The notes of the second slide at offset n2: a record of type 0x03F0 (version 15) holding a record of type 0x03F1 with `le` of 257 (4) and 4 zero bytes and `pdraw` of one shape `pbox(0, ...)` with `ptext(2, true, "Second notes")`. (5) The notes of the first slide at offset n1: the same without the shape and with `le` of 256 in the notes atom, and `pdraw` of nothing. (6) The first persist directory at offset pa: a record of type 0x1772 whose data is `le` of 1 plus 5 times 1048576 (4 bytes: first id 1, count 5) and then the five offsets 0, s1, s2, n2 and n1 (4 bytes each). (7) The first edit at offset ea: a record of type 0x0FF5 with data: `le` of 256 (4), 4 zero bytes, `le` of 0 (4, no earlier edit), `le` of pa (4), `le` of 1 (4, the document id), `le` of 7 (4), 4 zero bytes. (8) A later version of the second slide at offset s2b: the layout of the second slide with the text "Edited later". (9) The second persist directory at offset pb: type 0x1772, data `le` of 3 plus 1 times 1048576 (4) and `le` of s2b (4). (10) The second edit at offset eb: type 0x0FF5 with data: `le` of 256 (4), 4 zero bytes, `le` of ea (4), `le` of pb (4), `le` of 1 (4), `le` of 7 (4), 4 zero bytes. The stream u ("Current User") is a record header (`le` of 0 (2), `le` of 0x0FF6 (2), `le` of 20 (4)), then `le` of 20 (4), `le` of token (4), `le` of eb (4) and 4 zero bytes. Build the compound file with `cfbmk::cm_reset`, `cfbmk::cm_add("Current User", u)`, `cfbmk::cm_add("PowerPoint Document", d)` and `cfbmk::cm_build(false, 109, out)`.

`putps(out byte[>..]&, id int, ty int, data const byte[:])` appends to out the 16-byte entry of a message property stream for a fixed-size value: `le` of ty (2), of id (2), of 0 (4, flags), then data padded with zero bytes to 8.

`msgprops(natt int, out byte[>..]&)` clears out and builds the property stream of a message: 8 zero bytes, `le` of 0 and of natt (4 bytes each, the next ids), `le` of 0 (4, recipients) and of natt (4, attachments), 8 zero bytes, then `putps` with id 0x0039, type 0x0040 and the 8 bytes `le` of 134168328000000000 (8 bytes).

`ustream(name const byte[:], s const byte[:])` adds to the compound file being built the stream whose name is name and whose content is s as UTF-16 (`txt` with wide true), with `cfbmk::cm_add`.

`msgbuild(kind int, out byte[>..]&)` clears out and builds a message file with `cfbmk::cm_reset` and `cfbmk::cm_build(false, 109, out)`. Kind 1 (full message): `cm_add("__properties_version1.0", ...)` with `msgprops(2, ...)`, then `ustream` of "__substg1.0_0037001F" "Invented subject", "__substg1.0_0C1A001F" "Ada Example", "__substg1.0_5D01001F" "ada@example.invalid", "__substg1.0_0E04001F" "Ben Sample; Cy Test", "__substg1.0_0E03001F" "Di Copy" and "__substg1.0_1000001F" "Hello team." followed by the bytes 13 and 10 (use `txt` of the text, then the two bytes as units) and "Second line."; the first attachment: `ustream` of "__attach_version1.0_#00000000/__substg1.0_3707001F" "report.txt", `ustream` of "__attach_version1.0_#00000000/__substg1.0_370E001F" "TEXT/Plain" and `cm_add("__attach_version1.0_#00000000/__substg1.0_37010102", ...)` with the 6 bytes "abc123"; the second attachment: `cm_add("__attach_version1.0_#00000001/__substg1.0_3704001E", ...)` with the 7 bytes "pic.png", `ustream` of "__attach_version1.0_#00000001/__substg1.0_370E001F" "image/png" and `cm_add("__attach_version1.0_#00000001/__substg1.0_37010102", ...)` with the 4 bytes 137, 80, 78 and 71. Kind 2 (HTML only): `msgprops(0, ...)`, `ustream` of "__substg1.0_0037001F" "Page", "__substg1.0_0C1A001F" "Eve Only" and `cm_add("__substg1.0_10130102", ...)` with the bytes of "<html><body><p>Hi <b>there</b></p></body></html>". Kind 3 (RTF only): `msgprops(0, ...)`, `ustream` of "__substg1.0_0037001F" "Rich" and `cm_add("__substg1.0_10090102", ...)` with the 4 bytes "xxxx". Kind 4 (nothing but a subject): `msgprops(0, ...)` and `ustream` of "__substg1.0_0037001F" "Bare".

`attsum()` prints one line for each attachment i of `ole::ol_att_count()`: "att ", i, a space, the name from `ole::ol_att_name`, a space, the type from `ole::ol_att_type`, a space, the number of data bytes from `ole::ol_att_data` and then a space and each of its first 4 bytes as decimal numbers separated by spaces (as many as exist).

`runm(label const byte[:], file const byte[:])` calls `dm::dm_reset("msg", "part")`, `dump(label, ole::ol_read(file))` and `attsum()`.

`runw(label const byte[:], file const byte[:], kind const byte[:], unit const byte[:])` calls `dm::dm_reset(kind, unit)` and `dump(label, ole::ol_read(file))`.

`wrong(label const byte[:], data const byte[:])` calls `dm::dm_reset("x", "y")` and prints the label, a space, and 1 or 0 for whether `ole::ol_read(data)` returned true, then one "warn " line for each line of `dm::dm_warn`.

`main()` first calls `show` with these file names, kinds and units: "sample.doc" ("doc", "section"), "legacy.doc" ("doc", "section"), "edge.doc" ("doc", "section"), "boxes.doc" ("doc", "section"), "sample.xls" ("xls", "sheet"), "legacy.xls" ("xls", "sheet"), "edge1900.xls" ("xls", "sheet"), "edge1904.xls" ("xls", "sheet"), "sample.ppt" ("ppt", "slide"), "legacy.ppt" ("ppt", "slide"). Then, each with a scratch list and `runw` or `runx` as named: `wbbuild(false, ...)` with `runx("synth1900", "Workbook", ...)`; `wbbuild(true, ...)` with `runx("synth1904", "Workbook", ...)`; `oldbook` with `runx("biff5", "Book", ...)`; `lockedbook` with `runx("locked", "Workbook", ...)`; for `bigbook`, call `cfile("Workbook", ...)`, `dm::dm_reset("xls", "sheet")`, read it, write the Markdown from `dm::dm_markdown` into a scratch list, and print "== big", "len " and its length, "ok " and 1 or 0 for the reader result and one "warn " line for each line of `dm::dm_warn`; then `wdbuild(0xC1, 0x200, "1Table", ...)` with `runw("synthdoc", ..., "doc", "section")`; `wdbuild(0xC1, 0, "0Table", ...)` with `runw("synthdoc0", ...)`; `wdbuild(0xC1, 0x300, "1Table", ...)` with `runw("lockeddoc", ...)`; `wdbuild(0x65, 0x200, "1Table", ...)` with `runw("olddoc", ...)`; `ppbuild(0xE391C05F, ...)` with `runw("synthppt", ..., "ppt", "slide")`; `ppbuild(0xF3D1C4DF, ...)` with `runw("lockedppt", ...)`; `msgbuild(1, ...)`, `msgbuild(2, ...)`, `msgbuild(3, ...)`, `msgbuild(4, ...)` with `runm("msg1", ...)` to `runm("msg4", ...)`; the file made by `cfile("EncryptedPackage", the bytes of "data", ...)` with `runw("encpkg", ..., "x", "y")`. Last, "== wrong formats": `wrong` of "empty" with no bytes, of "text" with the bytes of "hello world", of "unknown" with `cfile("Foo", "bar", ...)`, of "cut" with the first 3000 bytes of the fixture "tests/fixtures/sample.doc" read with `os::read_file`, of "zip" with the fixture "tests/fixtures/edge.docx".

